# Ticket state contract

One file per ticket or local QA task: `tickets/{ID}.md`. Free text uses a
collision-free `LOCAL-<number>` and `tracker: null`. Preserve prior evidence.

## Required frontmatter

```yaml
---
id: LOCAL-1
title: Verify the agreed feature behavior
scope: other
status: analyzing
verdict: null
build: null
environment: null
tracker: null
design: null
source_refs: []
started: 2026-09-24
updated: 2026-09-24
next_action: "Resolve the target build and independent expected result"
blockers: []
---
```

Scopes: api, ui-web, mobile, backend, desktop, data, device, other,
multi-surface, tooling. Environment is a nonempty team-specific name or null;
its label is not evidence of safety. Build/design are strings or null; tracker
is an HTTP(S) URL or null. Dates are ISO YYYY-MM-DD; updated cannot precede started.
source_refs/blockers are lists of nonempty strings. Pin source commit in the body,
not only paths/line numbers.

Optional fields: `owner`, `branch` (string or null); `env_role` (dev, test, staging,
production, unknown); `evidence` (list of local artifact paths relative to the ticket
file). Team ownership is coordination, not a lock. Keep remote evidence links in
the body with the result excerpt; CI cannot authenticate private artifact URLs.

The stdlib validator supports a flat YAML mapping, plain/JSON-double-quoted or
YAML-single-quoted scalar values, and inline or indented scalar lists. Comments
and CRLF are supported. Quote values containing colon-space or special syntax.
Nested mappings, block scalars, tags, anchors and duplicate keys are rejected.

## Lifecycle and verdict

`analyzing → analyzed → planned → in_test → reporting → done`; any active state
may become blocked with a reason and an executable next_action. Record access
blockers early, before tests exist. Resume at the earliest affected phase; changed
build/config/requirements invalidate only relevant prior claims. Reopening done
starts a dated run, sets verdict null, and keeps previous evidence.

Verdict is null while active; done requires pass, partial or fail. Confirmed
in-scope defects mean fail. Missing required coverage without a confirmed defect
means partial. Pass requires agreed checks executed successfully, no unresolved
in-scope failures, and meaningful evidence. Status done means this run has a
handoff, not that the product is defect-free. No fabricated source or run output.

`tools/tickets/validate.py` checks schema and the *presence* of local evidence or
an execution block for pass. It cannot establish artifact truth, assertion quality
or sufficient coverage. A two-line block is not permission to invent a run.

## Body and update procedure

Analysis (criteria/oracle, source risks or gap, observed interfaces) → Test plan
(risk/technique/layer/expected result and automated/manual/blocked status) →
Execution & results (command/actions, time, target/build/config, results, artifacts,
failed attempts, skips) → Handoff (owner/branch, dirty paths, remaining scope,
next action) → Tracker comment (draft/posted destination and ID) → Notes.

Update status, updated, next_action and blockers at every phase boundary.
Serialize writers. Validate the ticket, then run `python3 tools/tickets/index.py`.
The index is derived data; do not resolve conflicts by editing it manually.
